---
// web/src/pages/legal/privacy/index.astro
// /legal/privacy/, the privacy policy (Phase 5 Step 16; ROADMAP §5 "Privacy";
// ADR-0013 decision 20 and "Personal data"), generated from
// src/content/legal/data-flows.json: each flow (what is collected, why, on
// what basis, by which processor, where and for how long) and each processor
// with its data processing agreement. tests/unit/legal.test.ts holds the file
// complete against the Content Security Policy, and tests/web/test_data_flows.py
// against ADR-0013's table and the hosts and logs the service keeps, so a flow
// cannot exist without its row here. The short version up top states the
// promises the tests prove: no cookie, no account, the calculator's inputs in
// a POST body that is neither logged nor stored, a user's file parsed in the
// browser and never uploaded, and Cloud Run's request logs excluded. An open
// question for counsel is marked beside its flow.
import Breadcrumbs from "@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import CounselNote from "@/components/CounselNote.astro";
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, DATA_FLOWS, LEGAL_INDEX, OPERATOR, PRIVACY, processorOf } from "@/lib/legal";
import { webPage, type Crumb } from "@/lib/structured-data";

const trail: Crumb[] = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: LEGAL_INDEX.title, path: LEGAL_INDEX.path },
  { name: PRIVACY.title, path: PRIVACY.path },
];
const h2 = "mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold";
const p = "mt-3 max-w-3xl";
---

<Base
  title={PRIVACY.title}
  description={PRIVACY.description}
  jsonLd={webPage(PRIVACY.path, PRIVACY.title, PRIVACY.description, trail)}
  searchable
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={trail} />
  <span hidden data-pagefind-meta="kind:Legal"></span>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">{PRIVACY.title}</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Last updated {PRIVACY.updated}</p>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-lg">
    pyeconomics.dev is operated by {OPERATOR}, who decides what happens to the personal data described here (the
    controller). Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with any question or request.
  </p>

  <h2 id="short" class={h2}>The short version</h2>
  <ul class="mt-3 max-w-3xl list-disc pl-6" data-promises="">
    <li>No cookies, no accounts, no advertising, and no profiles. Nothing is sold or shared for advertising.</li>
    <li>
      The calculator sends your inputs with POST, in the request's body, so they never appear in a URL. The server neither
      logs nor stores a run's body, and tells every cache not to keep the answer.
    </li>
    <li>
      A file you use for "Use my own data" is read in your browser, in a Web Worker, and never uploaded: only the values
      you map into the form travel, in the run's body.
    </li>
    <li>
      Cloud Run's automatic request logs, which would record each URL, your IP address and your browser, are excluded
      before they are stored. The service keeps its own records of each request's route, status and duration only, for
      30 days, and no URL.
    </li>
    <li>Analytics count visits without cookies and honour Do Not Track; the local app counts nothing.</li>
    <li>Your email address is kept only if you sign up for the newsletter or the Pro waitlist, and confirm it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="flows" class={h2}>What is collected, why, and for how long</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Each way personal data could reach pyeconomics or the services it uses, with who processes it, where it is kept and
    for how long. A processor handles the data only for pyeconomics, under the data processing agreement named in the
    next section.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-6">
    {
      DATA_FLOWS.flows.map((flow) => {
        const processor = processorOf(flow);
        return (
          <section class="rounded-lg border p-4" aria-labelledby={`flow-${flow.id}`} data-flow={flow.id}>
            <h3 id={`flow-${flow.id}`} class="text-lg font-semibold">
              {flow.title}
            </h3>
            <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm sm:grid-cols-[10rem_1fr]">
              <dt class="font-medium">What</dt>
              <dd>{flow.collected}</dd>
              <dt class="font-medium">Why</dt>
              <dd>{flow.purpose}</dd>
              <dt class="font-medium">Lawful basis</dt>
              <dd>{flow.basis}</dd>
              <dt class="font-medium">Who processes it</dt>
              <dd data-processor={flow.processor ?? ""}>
                {processor ? <a href={`#processor-${processor.id}`}>{processor.name}</a> : "No one: it stays on your device"}
              </dd>
              <dt class="font-medium">Where</dt>
              <dd>{flow.location}</dd>
              <dt class="font-medium">How long</dt>
              <dd>{flow.retention}</dd>
            </dl>
            {flow.counsel && <CounselNote question={flow.counsel} />}
          </section>
        );
      })
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="processors" class={h2}>Processors and their agreements</h2>
  <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
    {
      DATA_FLOWS.processors.map((processor) => (
        <li id={`processor-${processor.id}`} class="scroll-mt-24 rounded-lg border p-4 text-sm" data-processor-entry={processor.id}>
          <p class="text-base font-semibold">{processor.name}</p>
          <p class="mt-1">For {processor.service}.</p>
          <p class="mt-1">
            Agreement: <a href={processor.dpa.url} rel="noopener noreferrer">{processor.dpa.basis}</a>.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1">
            <a href={processor.privacy} rel="noopener noreferrer">
              {processor.name}'s privacy policy
            </a>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 id="transfers" class={h2}>Where the data goes</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    pyeconomics and most of its processors are in the United States, and so is most of the data described here. Where
    personal data from the European Union, the European Economic Area, Switzerland or the United Kingdom goes to the
    United States, the processors' agreements rely on the European Commission's standard contractual clauses (and the
    United Kingdom's addendum) or an equivalent safeguard.
  </p>
  <CounselNote question="whether pyeconomics needs a representative in the EU or the UK, and the safeguards each transfer relies on." />

  <h2 id="rights" class={h2}>Your rights</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Wherever you live, you can ask what personal data pyeconomics holds about you, and ask for it to be corrected or
    deleted. If you are in the EU, the EEA, Switzerland or the UK, you also have the rights the GDPR and the UK GDPR give
    you: to access, rectify and erase your data, to restrict or object to its processing, to data portability, and to
    withdraw consent at any time without affecting what was done before. You can complain to your data protection
    authority. In California and other US states, you have the rights their privacy laws give you; pyeconomics does not
    sell or share personal data as those laws define it.
  </p>
  <p class={p}>
    To use a right, write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. You will have an answer within a
    month. Most of what is described here holds no name, so pyeconomics may not be able to find data about you unless
    you tell it what to look for, such as the email address you signed up with. To leave the newsletter or the waitlist,
    use the unsubscribe link in any email; to have your address deleted from the email processor too, write to the
    address above.
  </p>

  <h2 id="children" class={h2}>Children</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    pyeconomics is not directed at children under 16 and does not knowingly collect their personal data. If you believe
    a child has signed up, write to the address above and the address will be deleted.
  </p>

  <h2 id="security" class={h2}>Security</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Every connection to pyeconomics.dev is encrypted (HTTPS, with HSTS). The service keeps no input, file or key, the
    snapshot bucket is private, and the service's secrets live in a secret manager. Report a vulnerability through the
    <a href="https://github.com/pyeconomics-dev/pyeconomics/blob/main/SECURITY.md">security policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes" class={h2}>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    This policy is generated from the same file that the site's tests check against what the site and the service
    really do. When a flow or a processor changes, the policy changes with it, in the same change to the repository, and
    the date at the top moves. The repository keeps every earlier version.
  </p>
</Base>
